      <!-- Detailed Review: CINC -->
      <div id="cinc" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:16px;padding:32px;margin-bottom:64px;">
        <h3 style="color:white;font-size:1.3rem;margin-bottom:16px;">CINC</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          CINC is an all in one real estate lead generation and CRM platform, and it is the platform teams graduate to when they want bought leads rather than another database. Every plan bundles buyer leads from managed Google and Facebook campaigns, a branded IDX website with property search, saved searches and alerts, landing pages, a Seller Suite, video messaging, mass messaging, database imports and integrations including Zapier, BombBomb and the Mojo dialer. Its advertising is genuinely specialised: hyperlocal targeting by neighbourhood, school district and property type, shifting up to cash offer ads and Google Local Service Ads for seller leads, and it reports managing more than $30 million a year in ad spend. Capterra rates it 4.1 out of 5 from 249 reviews, and the happy reviews are concrete about why teams stay. One 10 year customer wrote that "CINC is the main reason that my commissions have almost tripled since I began with them." Another, on the CRM itself, said: "Great dashboard on the CRM and easy to navigate. Very consistent lead flow month over month."
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          Where it costs more than the headline, and this is where we have to be straight with you. CINC does not publish its plan prices at all, so what follows are third party reports rather than vendor confirmed figures. On CINC's own pricing page the four tiers, Solo for 1 user, Ramp for up to 4, Pro for up to 49 and Select for 50 or more, all say Request Pricing, and CINC's FAQ answers how much it costs with an invitation to contact the team. The only prices on that page are add-ons: CINC AI from $200 a month and a dialer at 3 lines for $75 a month. Independent roundups and pricing indexes agree closely on the rest, starting near $899 a month for a solo agent and $1,299 to $1,500 a month for a team, with buyer leads included and the software not sold separately from lead generation. On top of that sits a mandatory co-managed ad budget, typically starting around $500 a month, and a reported 6 month minimum commitment that commonly runs 6 to 12 months. One industry review calculated true out of pocket costs at $1,500 to $3,000 or more per month, and another put a properly run solo setup at $1,800 to $3,500 a month. Annual contracts, onboarding fees and an early exit that requires paying out the remaining term are all reported parts of the deal, so the number that matters is the one on your order form.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          The reviews split hard, and the fault line is lead quality. Capterra's lower rated reviews are blunt. A reviewer with more than two years on the platform wrote that "The cost of CINC is significant, and for me, it simply wasn't worth it," and added: "My advice: Don't Do It! Don't Sign the Contract! Put Your Efforts, Energy, Time &amp; Money elsewhere!!" A broker from a 10 year relationship said the platform alone had reached $1450 a month and that "The pricing is too high when there are a hundred other services offering the same thing for half the price." On the leads themselves, one reviewer said: "They brag about scrubbing leads and only supplying valid emails and phone numbers. However, well over 20% of the phone numbers are for non-existent people," and concluded that "The leads are almost all duds, I purchased the CINC program to increase my business, but the only thing it has done is draw over $1300 per month out of my business." A one star reviewer put it plainly: "Its hard to do when the phone numbers and emails and even the names are wrong. I would never ever suggest Cinc." Smaller frictions repeat too, including "The mobile app is limited and then using the CINC website on Safari is even worse" and a complaint that "any suggestions for improvements or constructive criticism on changes which have been made are simply ignored." It is only fair to record CINC's side: in public replies the company points out that paid search and paid social leads are not referrals, that they need training, fast calling and time to warm up, and that agents who work the system well do convert. That is an honest description of how bought leads behave. It is also the reason CINC is a poor fit for an agent with no time to call, because the platform hands you a lead and a script, not a conversation.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          The structural point is ownership. Everything you build on CINC, the IDX website, its pages, its search experience and whatever search traction those URLs earn, sits on infrastructure CINC controls. Cancel and the site goes dark, the pages and their rankings go with it, and because CINC does not sell the platform without its lead generation, the lead flow stops at the same moment. If paid advertising is how you want to meet strangers, you have a defined farm area, you can carry the ad budget comfortably for twelve months and you have someone whose job is answering the phone fast, CINC is a real business and its training and ad targeting are genuinely good. If you want the phone answered, the follow-up handled and a website and prospect list you actually own, read the order form carefully before you sign one.
        </p>
