      <!-- FAQ -->
      <h2 id="faq" style="font-size:1.6rem;font-weight:700;color:white;margin-bottom:16px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div style="margin-bottom:48px;">

        <details style="padding:20px;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:12px;margin-bottom:12px;">
          <summary style="color:white;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;">
            <span>How much does CINC cost?</span>
            <span style="font-size:0.9rem;">▼</span>
          </summary>
          <div style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;">
            CINC does not publish its plan prices. On its own pricing page all four tiers, Solo, Ramp, Pro and Select, say Request Pricing, and CINC's FAQ answers the cost question with an invitation to contact the team. What is published are the add-ons: CINC AI starts at $200 a month and a dialer is 3 lines for $75 a month. Third party reporting is consistent that the platform starts around $899 a month for a solo agent and $1,299 to $1,500 a month for a team, with buyer leads included, plus a mandatory co-managed ad budget that typically starts near $500 a month. By contrast BirkyStudio publishes every price: The Base Agent is $1,297 solo (Notion CRM included), the Text Receptionist is $297, the Voice Receptionist is $550 plus Vapi usage of about $30 to $50 a month, and websites are $499, $999 or $1,999 one-time.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details style="padding:20px;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:12px;margin-bottom:12px;">
          <summary style="color:white;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;">
            <span>Does CINC answer my phone or reply on WhatsApp?</span>
            <span style="font-size:0.9rem;">▼</span>
          </summary>
          <div style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;">
            No. CINC is a lead generation and CRM platform. It captures leads on an IDX website CINC builds and runs paid Google and Facebook ads to fill, then routes those leads into its CRM. It does not pick up an inbound phone call and it does not hold a WhatsApp conversation, and CINC AI is an add-on for AI assisted follow up inside the platform rather than a voice receptionist. BirkyStudio's Voice Receptionist is $550 one-time plus Vapi usage of about $30 to $50 a month and answers calls 24/7 in English and Spanish, and the WhatsApp Text Receptionist is $297 one-time.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details style="padding:20px;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:12px;margin-bottom:12px;">
          <summary style="color:white;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;">
            <span>Does CINC require a long term contract?</span>
            <span style="font-size:0.9rem;">▼</span>
          </summary>
          <div style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;">
            CINC is reported to require a minimum commitment of 6 months, and agreements commonly run 6 to 12 months or a full year. Industry reporting says the terms generally require paying out the remainder of the term rather than cancelling at will, so if you want out at month four you may owe the balance. CINC does not publish the term either, so it lives on the order form. BirkyStudio has no contract and no commitment: you pay once for a build, and there is a 30 day money back guarantee on it.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details style="padding:20px;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:12px;margin-bottom:12px;">
          <summary style="color:white;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;">
            <span>Are CINC's leads good?</span>
            <span style="font-size:0.9rem;">▼</span>
          </summary>
          <div style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;">
            CINC's own reviews are the fairest answer, and they split sharply. Capterra rates it 4.1 out of 5 from 249 reviews, and happy users describe consistent volume and closed deals, with one long time customer writing that CINC is the main reason their commissions have almost tripled. The recurring complaint is lead quality. A reviewer who stayed more than two years wrote that the cost is significant and simply was not worth it, another said the leads are almost all duds while over $1300 a month left the business, and a one star reviewer said the phone numbers, emails and even the names were wrong. CINC's public reply to those reviews points out that paid search and paid social leads need training, fast calling and time to warm up, which is a fair description of how the channel works. BirkyStudio's answer is a verified, enriched, scored prospect list you own, $497 Starter or $997 Pro one-time, with no bots and no dead numbers.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details style="padding:20px;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:12px;margin-bottom:12px;">
          <summary style="color:white;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;">
            <span>Does CINC include a website, and do I own it?</span>
            <span style="font-size:0.9rem;">▼</span>
          </summary>
          <div style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;">
            Yes, a branded IDX website with property search, saved searches and alerts is part of the CINC subscription, and it is genuinely one of the reasons teams buy. What you do not get is ownership. The site is hosted and managed by CINC, so the pages, the search experience and the SEO equity built on those URLs belong to the platform, and cancelling takes the site with it. BirkyStudio builds a website you own for $499 for a 1 to 3 page Starter site, $999 for a Professional 4 to 7 page bilingual site, or $1,999 for a Premium 10+ page site with a blog. The code is yours.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details style="padding:20px;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:12px;margin-bottom:12px;">
          <summary style="color:white;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;">
            <span>Is BirkyStudio cheaper than CINC?</span>
            <span style="font-size:0.9rem;">▼</span>
          </summary>
          <div style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;">
            On a realistic all in basis, yes, by a wide margin. Third party reporting puts a properly run solo CINC setup at $1,800 to $3,500 a month once the platform fee, the mandatory $500 a month ad budget and the usual add-ons are stacked, which is $21,600 to $42,000 in year one. BirkyStudio has no monthly platform fee at all: the Base Agent is $1,297 one-time with the Notion CRM included, the Voice Receptionist is $550 one-time plus about $30 to $50 a month in Vapi usage, and the only recurring line is optional care at $97 a month (Care and Maintenance) or $197 a month (Business AI Partner). Year one with the Base Agent and the care plan is about $2,461, and year two is only the care plan.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details style="padding:20px;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:12px;margin-bottom:12px;">
          <summary style="color:white;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;">
            <span>What happens to my leads and my website if I cancel CINC?</span>
            <span style="font-size:0.9rem;">▼</span>
          </summary>
          <div style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;">
            Contacts can usually be exported, though reviewers report that notes, tags and behavioural history do not come across cleanly, and because CINC does not sell the platform without its lead generation, cancelling also ends the lead flow. The bigger loss is the website: every neighbourhood page, blog post and landing page you built on a CINC site sits at a URL CINC controls, so when the subscription ends the pages go dark, the links pointing at them break, and any search traction those specific URLs earned has to be rebuilt at new addresses. BirkyStudio builds the site on your own account, and the prospect list is a file you keep, so both stay with you if you ever stop working with us.
          </div>
        </details>

      </div>

